{"id": 31, "ts": 1783951436.0278633, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I appreciate the panel\u2019s focus on the missing stress\u2011resilience and decision\u2011quality data for all candidates. After probing the other recruiters, we still have no observations of Candidate\u202fC\u2019s behavior under acute stress, nor of Candidate\u202fB\u2019s decision quality in safety\u2011critical moments, and we lack concrete evidence of Candidate\u202fD\u2019s crew\u2011communication style. Given this, we must weigh the evidence we do have.\nCandidate\u202fC demonstrates the strongest technical profile for long\u2011distance operations: the ability to anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, and possess excellent spatial vision, all of which underpin situational awareness on international routes. Moreover, C\u2019s very good leadership qualities suggest the capacity to guide the crew effectively. The interpersonal concerns\u2014being seen as a show\u2011off and not open to new ideas\u2014are notable but less directly safety\u2011critical than the communication deficits of Candidate\u202fA, the hostile remarks of Candidate\u202fB, and the hot\u2011tempered, know\u2011it\u2011all attitude of Candidate\u202fD, each of which could jeopardize crew cooperation and professional communication.\nTherefore, despite the lack of stress\u2011resilience evidence, I maintain my provisional recommendation for **Candidate\u202fC**, while acknowledging that we should seek a formal stress\u2011resilience assessment before finalizing the hire."}
